    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane",
    "problem": "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=y-x{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,1,2).$",
    "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1) & =-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1) & =1-2(-1)(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1) \\\\ z-2 & =-(x+1)+3(y-1) \\\\ z-2 & =-x+3y-4 \\\\ x-3y+z+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
